# Project Structure

1. Component Creation
Create all components as React components in the src\business-apps folder.
Each component should extend its props from the `BusinessAppContextProps` interface.

All folders in src\business-apps folder are products that we have, e.g. crm, irm etc.
All folders in src\business-apps\<product> are either named `components`, `modules`, `hooks`, `libs`, `utils`, `overview-screens`, `detail-screens`, `flow-screens` or contain the name of the component.
All files in src\business-apps\<product>\<screenName> are files related to that screen where one `.tsx` with the name of screen is located and an `index.ts` file with a named export of the component and a default export of the component.
Example of correctly create components:
- src\business-apps\irm\detail-screens\LeadDetail\LeadDetail.tsx
- src\business-apps\irm\overview-screens\LeadOverview\LeadOverview.tsx
- src\business-apps\crm\overview-screens\OpportunityOverview\OpportunityOverview.tsx

2. Component Development
Use ant-design for React UI components
Use typescript for type safety
Use prettier for code styling
Use eslint for linting

Always prefix CSS all classes with the `gba:` prefix.

Do not install other packages for UI themes, icons, etc unless absolutely necessary or I request them.

3. Component Publishing
Use npm run publish:screen -- src/business-apps/**/<ScreenName> to publish a screen

# Project Development

1. Git Commit Message Structure
Using the following format for Git commit messages:

<type>[optional scope]: <description>
[optional body]
[optional footer(s)]
Start with a type and the issue key as the scope (in brackets) followed colon and a meaningful description of the changes.

For example:
ci(PPLAT-2190): Enable Sonar Scan for the project

## List of types

- fix: a commit of the type fix patches a bug in your codebase (this correlates with PATCH in Semantic Versioning).
- feat: a commit of the type feat introduces a new feature to the codebase (this correlates with MINOR in Semantic Versioning).
- build: Changes that affect the build system or external dependencies 
- ci: Changes to our CI configuration files and scripts 
- docs: Documentation only changes
- perf: A code change that improves performance
- refactor: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature
- style: Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (white-space, formatting, missing semi-colons, etc)
- test: Adding missing tests or correcting existing tests

A good commit message should follow this template original propose by Tim Pope.

## Commit Message Body Structure

- Capitalized, short (50 chars or less) summary
- More detailed explanatory text, if necessary.  Wrap it to about 72 characters or so.  In some contexts, the first line is treated as the subject of an email and the rest of the text as the body.  The blank line separating the summary from the body is critical (unless you omit the body entirely); tools like rebase can get confused if you run the two together.
- Write your commit message in the imperative: "Fix bug" and not "Fixed bug" or "Fixes bug."  This convention matches up with commit messages generated by commands like git merge and git revert.
- Further paragraphs come after blank lines.
- Bullet points are okay, too
- Typically a hyphen or asterisk is used for the bullet, followed by a   single space, with blank lines in between, but conventions vary here
- Use a hanging indent